                "entry_id": "41106ebb-226b-32f2-acfc-4f5689008976",
                "word": "onguligrade",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "8ab1f1de-7f7b-41df-b90c-55fd24dcc918",
                        "entry_id": "41106ebb-226b-32f2-acfc-4f5689008976",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "masculine",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "onguligrades"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e009467a-2bf8-3900-9f82-1e07f91ca92b",
                        "entry_id": "41106ebb-226b-32f2-acfc-4f5689008976",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Se dit des mammifères qui marchent sur leurs ongles, c’est-à-dire sur leurs sabots."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3378ec41-755a-45ff-b07e-db69312836a1",
                        "source_entry_id": "41106ebb-226b-32f2-acfc-4f5689008976",
                        "source_sense_id": "e009467a-2bf8-3900-9f82-1e07f91ca92b",
                        "target_lang_word": "unguligrade",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "unguligrade",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f0d35bf0-b898-3d82-8f54-1eec0bf21a5e",
                                        "entry_id": "92dc6265-d58d-3c9d-be25-9598113dbf3d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "That walks on hooves."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7c237c92-4b99-4201-a5d0-014749c1ec96",
                                                "sense_id": "f0d35bf0-b898-3d82-8f54-1eec0bf21a5e",
                                                "text": "It has perhaps not been observed hitherto that the terms Digitigrada, Unguligrada, and Plantigrada fully apply to the Arthropoda, or that the manner in which the animals treat the ground and the corresponding structure of their feet deserve special attention as distinctive characters of classes and orders in this series of the animal kingdom; yet it is so. […] All Crustacea (including the Myriapoda, which merely exhibit the Crustacean type modified for terrestrial life) are Unguligrada; and the same is the case with all larvæ of Insects with complete metamorphosis, and with some of the lower groups of Insects, especially Thysanoura and Siphunculata—the unguligrade Arthropoda being at the same time, with few exceptions, also Solipeda."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6d055674-10eb-4d6f-962e-0f351a2f219b",
                                                "sense_id": "f0d35bf0-b898-3d82-8f54-1eec0bf21a5e",
                                                "text": "That excessive development of the epidermis which gives rise to a nail takes place, in the Horse, not only upon the dorsal surface of the terminal joint of the digit, but upon its ventral surface and sides, and thus produces a hoof. The animal is supported by these greatly-developed nails, and hence is said to be unguligrade."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b5d66c56-4a4a-436d-a696-74a3a55c6ff6",
                                                "sense_id": "f0d35bf0-b898-3d82-8f54-1eec0bf21a5e",
                                                "text": "The subjects of this book are the animals I will designate as megaherbivores. […] Such animals have been colloquially designated pachyderms; but a thick skin is a minor feature, and it is their very large body size that sets these few species apart from the numerous smaller species of unguligrade herbivore that occupy a wide variety of terrestrial ecosystems today."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "unguligrade",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "75a8106a-e510-3557-a493-a7cd6a68f790",
                                        "entry_id": "4f8ee86f-f6f0-30c6-8eac-a59462bc5b8b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An animal that walks on hooves."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c96affd4-91a9-4144-bf20-ed81540a69c5",
                                                "sense_id": "75a8106a-e510-3557-a493-a7cd6a68f790",
                                                "text": "A cheetah is a digitigrade and moves faster than a horse, an unguligrade."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6b7c38c1-944d-4be5-871f-a2c3bfbc924b",
                                                "sense_id": "75a8106a-e510-3557-a493-a7cd6a68f790",
                                                "text": "Dr. [Philip] Sclater submitted a classification of the Ruminating Artio-dactyls to the Section, in which he separated the Antilocapra from the other Unguligrades."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a9a9181f-d03f-447b-a99e-883c6387e2fa",
                                                "sense_id": "75a8106a-e510-3557-a493-a7cd6a68f790",
                                                "text": "Unguligrades (L. ungula, hoof) have reduced the number of digits present in their limbs. The unguligrade foot makes contact with the ground at the tips of the most distal digits and hooves have replaced claws. Unguligrades include ungulates such as ruminants, horses and pigs."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
